    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Ashland originated in the early twentieth century within Tobucksy County of the Choctaw Nation in Indian Territory.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        13,
        22
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2",
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/ashland_oklahoma"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Postal officials chose the name Ashland in 1902 after rejecting the community's original request to be called Pearl City.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        19,
        21
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ashland%2C_Oklahoma"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The economy of Ashland initially revolved around cattle and cotton farming, peaking at around 300 residents by 1911.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        15
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/ashland_oklahoma"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Boll weevil infestations in the 1910s and 1920s devastated cotton production, forcing Ashland to shift toward ranching.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        16
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/ashland_oklahoma"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Ashland remains a quiet rural community today, emphasizing its historical agricultural roots in the Pittsburg County landscape.",
